**Ground** `{colors.green}`, `slide-pad`. **Composes** topbar (mono label + monogram-circle), display-hero, body lede, footline. **Focal** a 2-line Source Serif 4 `display-hero` in pink (one word in cream), left. **Chrome** mono topbar; mono footline. **Accent** pink display + the monogram circle. **Silence** ~50%. **Fixed** serif 500, flat, monogram 2px pink. **Free** title, which word is cream. **Density** low.

###### 2 · Topic Tiles (catalog · move: rotating-fill grid · cream · the dense frame)

**Ground** `{colors.cream}`, `slide-pad`. **Composes** topbar, headline-xl, 3–4× topic-tile. **Focal** a row of 6px tiles, fills rotating green / pink / cream-2-with-border, each a mono ordinal + serif title. **Chrome** mono topbar. **Accent** the tile fills (mix 3 of 4, never repeat one). **Silence** tight — the density exception. **Fixed** 6px radius, rotating fills, no shadow. **Free** tile titles, which fills. **Density** dense-exception.

###### 3 · KPI Stat (data · move: oversized figure · green)

**Ground** `{colors.green}`, `slide-pad`. **Composes** topbar, kpi-block (mono tag + 220px stat-figure + serif description), footline. **Focal** a Source Serif 4 `stat-figure` (~11.5cqw, +unit) in pink over a 2px pink rule. **Chrome** mono topbar + footline. **Accent** the pink figure. **Silence** ~55%. **Fixed** serif 500 figure, 2px accent rule, flat. **Free** the figure (from script), tag, description. **Density** low.

###### 4 · Statement (quote · move: display serif · cream · left)

**Ground** `{colors.cream}`, generous pad. **Composes** topbar, display, name (600), caption-mono role. **Focal** a 2-line Source Serif 4 `display` (~7.3cqw) in green; a 600-weight attribution name + mono role beneath. **Chrome** mono topbar. **Accent** none — the serif carries it. **Silence** ~55%. **Fixed** serif 500 display + 600 name only, no italic. **Free** quote, name, role. **Density** low.

###### 5 · Step Framework (process · move: 8px step tiles · cream/green)

**Ground** `{colors.cream}` (or green), `slide-pad`. **Composes** topbar, headline, 3–4× step-tile. **Focal** a row of 8px step tiles (2.5px border) — mono ordinal + serif 68px title + body + mono marker over a top rule; fills rotate cream-with-green-border / green / pink. **Accent** the tile fills. **Silence** moderate. **Fixed** 8px radius, 2.5px border, mono markers. **Free** steps, fills. **Density** standard.

###### 6 · Chart (data · move: bars + meta · green)

**Ground** `{colors.green}`, `slide-pad`. **Composes** topbar, headline, bars + 2px axis rules, meta-dl. **Focal** 56px pink/cream/green bars on inner-edge 2px rules with mono ticks; an optional 3-col meta-dl beneath. **Chrome** mono topbar. **Accent** the bar fills. **Silence** moderate. **Fixed** 56px bars, 3px tops, 2px axes, mono ticks. **Free** values (from script), labels. **Density** standard.

##### Composition Rules

###### Do

- Run every display in **Source Serif 4 weight 500**, opsz, negative-tracked, tight line-height; **body at 400**.
- Set every label/caption/axis/footline in **JetBrains Mono 500 uppercase, 0.08–0.18em**.
- Give every frame a **topbar** (mono label + monogram or counter); footline on data/cover.
- Pick **one dominant surface per frame** (max two tones); **rotate tile fills**, never repeat one across a grid.
- Separate sections with **2px hairlines** in the region's accent; reserve the monogram circle for cover/summary.
- Scale display aggressively (7.3–11.5cqw) for statement/stat/cover; lean left/editorial, one subject per frame.

###### Don't

- No box-shadow, gradient, or glow — flat, paper-based depth only.
- No third typeface; no italic/underline; no serif body at 500; no mono at headline scale.
- No fourth color family; no `rgba` surfaces; no 1px or 4px+ rules (2px / 2.5px only).
- No two competing content blocks; don't omit the topbar.
- Don't blow a headline edge-to-edge — step the ramp down.

##### Aspect-Ratio Behavior

| Treatment      | 16:9                             | 9:16                        | 1:1                      |
| -------------- | -------------------------------- | --------------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Cover          | display left, monogram top-right | display top, monogram below | display, monogram corner |
| Topic Tiles    | 3–4 across                       | stacked                     | 2×2                      |
| KPI Stat       | figure left                      | figure centered, taller     | centered                 |
| Statement      | quote left                       | quote stacked               | centered                 |
| Step Framework | 3–4 across                       | stacked                     | 2×2                      |
| Chart          | bars + meta                      | bars taller, meta stacks    | square chart             |

`slide-pad` holds on the short edge; re-step display above the 1.4cqw floor. Topbar spans the top
on every ratio; the monogram may drop to a corner.

##### Approved Real Entities

No real customers, logos, or vendors are defined in the source — render any such mark as a
placeholder. The monogram, footline strings, and tile counters carry per-deck identity; figures are content.

##### Numerals & Claims (hard rule)

Never invent figures, KPIs, dates, or counts at frame scale. Render slots as `— figure —`,
`{metric}`, `— %`. KPI blocks, bars, and meta values especially carry placeholders until the script
supplies them. Issue numbers / counters are decorative chrome.

##### Pre-Render Self-Audit

- **Squint** — one serif moment dominates; the surface block centers the eye.
- **Silence** — one subject per frame in deep negative space; only the topic-tile/step grids run dense.
- **Triad** — two surface tones max; headlines/body/labels use the correct per-surface color; no fourth family.
- **Type** — Source Serif 4 **500** display (opsz, negative-tracked) / **400** body; mono uppercase 0.08–0.18em; ≥1.4cqw floor.
- **Depth** — 0 shadow, 0 gradient; 2px hairlines; 6/8px radii; monogram the only full round.
- **Chrome** — topbar present on every frame; footline on data/cover.
- **Fabrication** — every numeral traces to the script, else placeholder.

##### Known Gaps

- **Motion intentionally out of scope.** frame.md specifies composition only; the source relies on `deck-stage.js` for scaling/nav, no transition spec.
- **Source Serif 4 (opsz 8..60) + JetBrains Mono via Google Fonts** — the optical-size axis is critical; a non-opsz fallback flattens the size-aware letterforms. CJK: LXGW WenKai (display) / Noto Serif SC (body) / Noto Sans Mono CJK (chrome).
- **9:16 / 1:1 are guidance**; verify the floor and that two-tone discipline holds per ratio.
- Bars, hairlines, tiles, and the monogram circle are CSS-only; no external imagery is required.
- **Contrast:** green-on-pink / pink-on-green only at display scale (84px+); keep small text cream-on-green or ink-on-cream.


### Reference file: heygen-hyperframes/hyperframes/references/captions.md

#### Captions

##### Words and timings

- `npx hyperframes transcribe <file> --json` and a HeyGen voiceover (`/media-use`) give every word's start and end. Work from those as `[{ "id": "w0", "text": "Hello", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.5 }, …]`.
- `npx hyperframes transcribe` defaults to the `small.en` whisper model, which **translates** non-English speech into English. Pass the model: `npx hyperframes transcribe <file> --model small.en` for English, `--model small --language <code>` otherwise, `--model medium.en` for speech over music. It writes `assets/transcript.json` (its JSON names the path) and overwrites it on the next run; `--to srt` or `--to vtt` writes a sidecar.
- For Chinese, Japanese and other unspaced scripts, local transcription returns a whole sentence as one "word", sometimes in the other script (Traditional for Simplified speech). Split each segment's text into short phrases and spread its time across them by character count; check the characters against what is said.
- Whisper invents "Thank you." over silence and repeats lines over a silent tail: drop words that start inside a final silence (`silencedetect=noise=-35dB:d=0.6`; real speech sits around −16 to −26 dB).
- Never put two words in one timed entry, and match transcript words to caption words by position, not text, or a repeated word takes the wrong time.
- Read the transcript before using it. More than 20% `♪` or `�` tokens, or nonsense words, means it failed: retry with a larger model, or take lyrics from the person. Drop non-word tokens before building groups.

##### Structure

- One sub-composition host with `data-track-kind="captions"` holds every caption group, so Studio shows them on one captions track.
- Ready-made styles: `npx hyperframes catalog --tag caption-style --json`. An installed one is source you can restyle.

##### Groups

- 2–3 words for high energy, 3–5 conversational, 4–6 calm. Break at a sentence end, a pause of 150 ms or more, or the word limit.
- One group visible at a time. A group opens at or before its first word's start and closes at or after its last word's end. A caption at exactly 0 s looks pre-existing: start 0.1–0.3 s in.
- Every group ends with a hard kill after its exit, or a stale group can stay on screen:

```js
tl.to(group, { opacity: 0, duration: 0.12, ease: "power2.in" }, end - 0.12);
tl.set(group, { opacity: 0, visibility: "hidden" }, end);
```

`tl.set` is an instant flip, so setting `visibility` on the group is fine; never tween it on a `.clip`.

##### Fit and place

- Size each group to fit: `window.__hyperframes.fitTextFontSize(text, { fontFamily, fontWeight, maxWidth })` returns `{ fontSize }`. Defaults: `maxWidth` 1600 landscape, 900 portrait; base 78, minimum 42, step 2. When a word scales up for emphasis, divide `maxWidth` by that scale.
- A full-width absolute container, centred with text alignment. Never `left: 50%; transform: translateX(-50%)`, which clips at the frame edges.
- `overflow: visible`, not `hidden`, which cuts off scaled words and glows.
- Landscape: 80–120 px from the bottom. Portrait: about 600–700 px from the bottom. Never over the speaker's face; inside the 80% title-safe box.
- Position each group absolutely. In a flex column a group hidden with `visibility: hidden` still takes space, so the next one lands lower. In a stack that accumulates, groups in one block share one exit time, or the stack reflows.
- Fade the group or its words, not both: the opacities multiply and read as a pop. A gradient or `background-clip: text` fill goes on the inner text span, not the transformed container.
- Over footage, choose the blend by the brightness (0–255) behind the text: under 60 `screen`, 60–180 `overlay`, over 180 `normal` with opaque colour. Plain subtitles above brightness 60 get a scrim sized to the text (30–40%), never a full-width bar.
- Footage may already carry burned-in subtitles only mid-clip: scan a 1 fps contact sheet before placing captions.

##### Text behind the speaker

- Stack the video, then the captions, then a cutout of the person from `npx hyperframes remove-background` (`/media-use`), ordered by `z-index`. Shake or punch in the video and the cutout together in one wrapper.
- `check` treats a `<video>` as opaque across its whole box, so a WebM cutout fails every caption behind it with `text_occluded`: put `data-layout-allow-occlusion` on the cutout and judge from `snapshot`.
- A big word behind the head reads best 30–55% covered: under about 15% it floats, over about 65% it is eaten. Re-lay out when a content word is 80% covered, or two adjacent words 50% each. Measure the rendered word boxes against the matte over each caption's own time window (heads move: one clip went from y=224 to y=96 in 8 s); estimates from character widths were wrong both ways.
- The matte model drops mic arms, lets large rigs near the body leak in, and flickers on held objects. Its first run downloads 168 MB, and it runs at about 2 fps at 1080p on CPU.


### Reference file: heygen-hyperframes/hyperframes/references/film.md

#### Films from a source: measured facts

What is specific to HyperFrames, or was measured on shipped films, when one video is made from a website, a script, a pull request, a music track, a real place or a short motion brief. The story comes first, from `story.md`; the shape of each kind of film is in `types/`. Motion craft is in `motion.md`, look in `look.md`, sound in `sound.md`, captions in `captions.md`.

##### Script and pacing

- TTS reads about 2.2 words a second. Keep a scene to about 19 words (9 s); at most two scenes may reach 26 words (12 s). 30–90 s is about 155 words or fewer; a whole film about 400 at most.
- A voiceover longer than its visuals makes the visuals pad with idle drift. Trim the script; never stretch the motion.
- Everything on screen in the first quarter of a scene, then frozen, reads as a slideshow. Reveal each piece when the voiceover names it, across the back half of the scene; write the voiceover as separate phrases so it has cue points.
- Up to about six short scenes, build them yourself in one context (about 9 min for five, against about 21 min split across sub-agents). Beyond that, give each worker two or three scenes and start them all at once; a second wave nearly doubles the time.

##### From a website

- `npx hyperframes capture <url> --json` writes `screenshots/full-page.png`: a 1x plate of the whole page, taken after the scroll pass with sticky and fixed elements made static, pixel-exact for a 1920-wide viewport travelling down it. It is skipped above 16384 px tall (use `scroll-*.png`); pushing in past 1:1 needs its own 2x capture of that region.
- To show the site as it is, use its screenshots. Rebuilding the page in HTML drifts from the real layout; rebuild only the part that moves, or overlay captured assets at measured positions.
- `--capture-budget` (default 120000 ms) bounds work after navigation; `--timeout` bounds navigation only. Videos are not downloaded: `capture --video <dir> --list`, then `--index N`. `asset-descriptions.md` has vision captions only when `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, `GEMINI_API_KEY` or `GOOGLE_API_KEY` is set.
- "very little text content" with an empty asset catalog means the capture is unusable even when it says `ok: true`. A `logo-*.svg` name is a guess from page structure, not proof.
- Brand tokens: link blue `#0000EE` and status reds and greens are never the accent; the accent is the colour that recurs across the most roles. Icon fonts (Font Awesome, icomoon, webflow-icons) are never the display or body face; a captured monospace goes to the mono role. Use only weights the brand font ships, or 600/700 render as fake bold. Every preset has a light ground: for a dark brand, swap ground and ink.
- Capture keeps each `@font-face` rule, `unicode-range` subsets included, in `extracted/page.html`; copying the font files alone loses which characters each covers. Name copied fonts by family, weight and style, or an upright and an italic "Regular" collide.

##### From a pull request

- `gh pr view --json files` stops at about 100 files: page `gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/N/files`.
- The PR author only opened it: credit commit authors, by commit count (`commits[].authors[]`); skip bots, lockfiles and `dist/`. Reviewers come as logins; names via `gh api users/<login> --jq .name`. TTS reads `@handles` aloud, so speak names.
- Never invent a version. Use the first non-draft release published at or after the merge; else the default branch's `package.json` version as "unreleased"; else only the PR URL.
- Length from size (additions + deletions), as a ceiling: ≲50 lines 20–40 s, 50–200 40–70 s, 200–600 70–110 s, ≳600 lines or 25+ files 110–180 s.
- Show 2–4 hunks of 4–12 lines; counts like +N/−M go on a stat card, not into the voiceover.
- The `code-*` registry blocks (`npx hyperframes catalog --query code --json`) are 1920×1080 scenes driven by `window.__TOKENS` (Shiki tokens, `github-dark`; `fontStyle` bit 1 italic, bit 2 bold) and `window.__BLOCK` (`{id, effect, seq, line?, duration}`). `code-diff` and `code-morph` need at least two states; `code-highlight` counts lines from 0 and `code-scroll` from 1; `code-typing` types at a fixed speed, so check characters × speed against the scene's duration. They reserve no caption band: inset the panel when captions are on.

##### On music

- Library music, HeyGen's included, comes as 15–30 s loops: loop the bed to the film with a 0.4 s fade-in and a 1.5 s fade-out. A track whose opening is a quiet build drains a short film: start from a stronger later section.
- Trust beat times only on rhythmic stretches; on calm music the tracker invents a metronome, often at double tempo (`npx hyperframes beats` reports `bpmConfidence`). Cut there on phrase and energy changes; `npx hyperframes audio --only sections,onsets,moments --json` finds them.
- A move that must land on a hit starts 40–190 ms early; a reaction starts 0–45 ms after. The hit itself is a 0 ms `tl.set`: easing a hit kills it. Scenes tile the track with no gaps.
- A word meant to be read holds at least one beat. Any single treatment goes stale after about 6 s: lengthen a section with a hold or a new treatment, never by slowing tweens. On rhythmic sections give footage one clip per strong beat; on calm ones one slow push from 1.0 to 1.08.

##### Maps and real places

- A real place (a city, a building, a route, a zoom from the globe to a street) is shown on real imagery. Vector outlines from Natural Earth or GeoJSON read as a diagram and lose the place; keep them for borders and labels drawn on top.
- Live tiles change between renders, so bake the camera move to video with `scripts/bake-basemap.mjs` (MapLibre over Esri satellite or Carto tiles; writes an all-intra MP4 and `<NAME>-coords.json`). From space down to a street: `PROJECTION=globe FROM="lng,lat" CENTER="lng,lat" ZSTART=1.85 ZEND=16` (Earth in a starfield turns from FROM to CENTER, then dives); `--help` lists the rest. It needs network for the tiles and takes well under a second a frame, so bake once, keep the MP4, and run one bake at a time. Draw borders, pins and labels as a live SVG from those coordinates, animating them only while the camera holds.
- Credit the imagery on screen ("Esri, Maxar, Earthstar Geographics" or "© CARTO, © OpenStreetMap") in small type on a soft dark backing, so it holds contrast over bright tiles; mark it `data-layout-ignore` so the type floor in `check` skips it.

##### Short motion pieces

- Kinetic type takes 3–7 English words or 4–12 Chinese characters per scene. A count-up runs 1.2–1.6 s, then holds, landing on a downbeat or a hard stop.
- A highlighter that wraps across lines: tween a custom property on a `background-size` band with `box-decoration-break: clone`. `getTotalLength()` can return 0 before layout; give line draw-ons a fallback.
- Hide a round-capped line with a gap longer than the path (`stroke-dasharray="100 120"`, `stroke-dashoffset="110"` on `pathLength="100"`), not dash = gap = offset: that leaves a dot at the end on some curves.
- A point in an image guessed directly misses by 16–24% of the frame. `scripts/locate.mjs` narrows it with a numbered 9×9 grid, then 6×6 on the crop, then a check render (about 2% error).


### Reference file: heygen-hyperframes/hyperframes/references/format.md

#### Composition format

The detail behind the contract in `SKILL.md`. Full references: `npx hyperframes docs compositions`, `docs data-attributes`, `docs troubleshooting`.

##### Shape of a project

```html
<!-- index.html: a normal document; declares variables, mounts scenes -->
<html
  data-composition-variables='[{"id":"headline","type":"string","label":"Headline","default":"…"}]'
>
  <body>
    <div
      id="root"
      data-composition-id="main"
      data-start="0"
      data-duration="12"
      data-width="1920"
      data-height="1080"
    >
      <div
        id="intro"
        data-composition-id="intro"
        data-composition-src="compositions/intro.html"
        data-start="0"
        data-duration="4"
        data-track-index="1"
        data-width="1920"
        data-height="1080"
      ></div>
    </div>
    <script>
      window.__timelines["main"] = gsap.timeline({ paused: true });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

```html
<!-- compositions/intro.html: everything inside <template> -->
<template>
  <style>
    #root {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
    }
  </style>
  <div id="root" data-composition-id="intro" data-width="1920" data-height="1080">
    <h1 id="headline" class="clip" data-start="0.4" data-duration="3" data-var-text="headline">
      …
    </h1>
  </div>
  <script>
    const tl = gsap.timeline({ paused: true });
    tl.fromTo(
      "#headline",
      { opacity: 0, filter: "blur(8px)" },
      { opacity: 1, filter: "blur(0px)", duration: 0.18, ease: "power4.out" },
      0.4,
    );
    window.__timelines["intro"] = tl;
  </script>
</template>
```

- Give the host, the scene root and the timeline key the same id. A host id that differs from its scene's makes each render wait 45 s per scene, then show static frames. Styles and scripts outside `<template>` are dropped.
- Size the scene root with `inset: 0`, never hard-coded pixels; the canvas is `data-width` × `data-height`.
- Keep ids unique across the assembled page (prefix them: `#intro-title`). A scene's timeline can't reach elements outside its file.
- Use `fromTo`, not `from`, inside a scene: the host re-seeks the scene each time it shows, and `from` records its start state once, so it drifts on seek-back.
- Never `add()` a scene's timeline to the root's; the runtime drives each scene itself, and nesting seeks it twice.

##### Attributes you'll use most

| Attribute                                      | Meaning                                                                                             |
| ---------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `data-start`, `data-duration`                  | Seconds, local to the file that declares the element                                                |
| `data-track-index`                             | Studio lane only; paint order is CSS `z-index`                                                      |
| `data-track-kind`                              | `graphics` or `captions` on scene hosts (video and audio come from the tag)                         |
| `data-composition-src`, `data-variable-values` | Mount a scene file; per-mount variable values (a JSON object)                                       |
| `data-media-start`                             | Offset into the source media (a trim)                                                               |
| `data-playback-rate`                           | Constant speed, 0.1–10                                                                              |
| `data-has-audio="true"`                        | A `<video>` keeps its own sound; a timed `<video>` with neither this nor `muted` fails lint         |
| `data-volume`, `data-fade-in`, `data-fade-out` | Gain (1 = 0 dB, up to 3.98 = +12 dB) and edge fades; envelopes use `data-automation` (`/media-use`) |
| `data-link`, `data-sync-origin`                | A video and audio edited as one (see Linked clips)                                                  |
| `data-hidden`                                  | Hidden in preview and render; reversible                                                            |
| `data-no-timeline`                             | On the root of a still composition: no timeline to wait for                                         |

##### Variables

- Declare an array of `{id, type, label, default}`: the video's own on `index.html`'s `<html>`, a scene or block file's on that file's `<html>` (or root element), where they stay inside each mount. Types: `string` (`placeholder`, `maxLength`), `number` (`min`, `max`, `step`, `unit`), `color`, `boolean`, `enum` (`options: [{value, label}]`).
- Bind without script: `data-var-text="id"`, `data-var-src="id"` (keep a real fallback `src` on media with audio), `var(--id)` in CSS; for logic, `window.__hyperframes.getVariables()` once at init.
- Values are an object keyed by id. A host's `data-variable-values` sets one mount; Studio's Variables panel and batch renders set only the root's variables, so to vary a block, change its host's values. Variables can't change the root's `data-duration`.

##### Fonts

- Bundled, so they resolve by name: Inter, Montserrat, Outfit, Nunito, Oswald, League Gothic, Archivo Black, Space Mono, IBM Plex Mono, JetBrains Mono, EB Garamond, Playfair Display, Source Code Pro, Noto Sans JP, Roboto, Open Sans, Lato, Poppins. Common system fonts (Helvetica, Arial, Times, Menlo and the like) are swapped for the nearest of these.
- The bundle only has Latin text at a few weights per family (Inter: 400, 700, 900). The compiler still fetches each of these families from Google Fonts for the other weights and scripts, and caches the result in `~/.cache/hyperframes/fonts`.
- Any other family needs `@font-face` in the file that uses it, or lint fails `font_family_without_font_face`: `src: url('assets/fonts/x.woff2')` for a file in the project (a capture's own fonts, or one you download there), or `local('Exact Name')` for a font installed on this machine.

##### Timing and the timeline

- A clip shows over `[start, start + duration)`: an end state landing exactly on its end is never rendered.
- Only a composition's root timeline is paused; timelines nested inside it are not. Never `tl.play()`.
- A scene's clock starts at 0 at its host's `data-start`; media `data-start` is local to its composition. `<video>` / `<audio>` without `data-duration` play their full length; an `<img>` without one lasts 3 s.
- A host's `data-duration` is the scene's slot: a shorter scene timeline holds its last frame, and a slot shorter than the video goes blank when it ends.
- Root children with `data-start` are laid out full-frame by the runtime; an untimed full-bleed background needs `position: absolute; inset: 0` or it collapses to nothing.
- A fill on the composition root is dropped when the video has shader transitions or HDR media; put it on a full-bleed child.
- `data-start="intro + 0.5"` starts after `intro` ends (spaces required). A typo, a missing id or a cycle silently resolves to 0, so snapshot what you time this way.
- A clip nested inside another timed element counts its `data-start` from the composition, not from that element, and shows only while that element shows. `timeline` lists it under its parent and warns `never visible` when the windows miss (`lint`: `nested_clip_never_visible`).
- `npx hyperframes timeline --json` lists every clip by kind with `absStart`, `absEnd`, `file`, `src`, `playbackRate`, `nested`, `host` and `durationSource`: read it instead of opening every file. It reads one level of nesting (a deeper scene shows `children=unread`); `laneError` means a `data-automation` or `data-fx-chain` that doesn't parse.

##### Adding media

Write what a Studio drop writes, so the clip behaves like one the person dropped:

- `id`, `class="clip"`, `data-start` (the playhead or the requested time, never a silent 0) and `data-track-index`. Video with sound: `playsinline data-has-audio="true"`; silent footage: `muted playsinline`. Audio: `data-volume="1"`.
- Images and video fill the frame: `position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0`, width and height equal to the canvas, `object-fit: contain`.
- `z-index` is the number of top-level clips already in that file plus one.
- Raise the root's `data-duration` to cover the new clip's end, or it never plays.

##### Linked clips

Studio's Detach audio writes a muted `<video>` and an `<audio>` of the same file sharing `data-link` and `data-sync-origin`; Studio moves, trims, splits and deletes them as one.

- Prefer one `<video data-has-audio="true">`; link only when the sound needs its own track, volume or effects.
- Every member keeps the same `data-start`, `data-duration`, `data-media-start` and `data-playback-rate`; retime them all together.
- Unlink by removing `data-link` from every member. Leave `data-sync-origin` alone while they are still one source.

##### Layout audit opt-outs

`check` measures boxes at sampled times; `overflow: hidden` does not silence a finding. Use the narrowest opt-out:

| Attribute                        | Silences                                                                                                                 |
| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `data-layout-allow-overflow`     | Overflow on the element and every descendant, including text-clipping checks: keep it on the smallest decorative wrapper |
| `data-layout-bleed="true"`       | One intentional crop of primary text                                                                                     |
| `data-layout-allow-overlap`      | Deliberate text layering, on that block only (not inherited)                                                             |
| `data-layout-allow-occlusion`    | An element meant to cover text                                                                                           |
